<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex Aureus of St. Emmeram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/870">870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Codex Aureus of St. Emmeram is an illuminated Gospel book written in gold ink, made in 870 for the Carolingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. Its jewelled golden cover is one of the finest surviving pieces of Carolingian goldsmiths' work. Given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-emmerams-abbey">St. Emmeram's Abbey</a> in Regensburg, it stayed there until 1811, when it passed to the Bavarian State Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-aureus-of-st-emmeram.jpg" alt="Codex Aureus of St. Emmeram" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image was made by the Bayerische Staatsbibliothek München and the work itself was made by Carolingian craftsmen in ca... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Aureus_Sankt_Emmeram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
